WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.530
From 2000 to 2021, about 1,613 earthquakes
between magnitude 5 and 5.9 struck

2
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.870
each year, on average.

3
00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.450
Magnitude 8 or more?

4
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:14.530
About 1.2 a year.

5
00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:21.090
Yet just two years, 2004 and 2010, make
up about 65 per cent of the earthquake

6
00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:25.390
deaths in USGS's list for 2000 to 2019.

7
00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.890
So should planners prepare for
the frequent small earthquakes,

8
00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:30.730
or the rare big ones?

9
00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:35.150
In this mission, you'll plot magnitude
against frequency on a log scale,

10
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.230
and work out what a
1-in-100-year event really means.

11
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.010
Then you'll read hazard
maps the way engineers do,

12
00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:44.650
and place events on a risk matrix.

13
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:48.510
Your mission: decide which earthquakes
planners should prepare for,

14
00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:50.210
and justify your choice.
